Q: flutter riverpod example not triggering state notification A: <p>ok, it looks to be an issue with the age of the course and the setup of the screens vs updates to Riverpod.  After much trial and error and Claude assistance, it appears that in Riverpod 3.0, background widgets don't auto-rebuild when navigating between screens. Riverpod 3.0 pauses listeners when widgets are not visible. Seems crazy to me, however, putting the following into the base screen solved the issue.  Hopefully someone here can explain this better so I know how to build my screens in a better way moving forward.</p>
<pre><code>class _TabsScreenState extends ConsumerState&lt;TabsScreen&gt; {
  int _selectedPageIndex = 0;

  @override
  void initState() {
    super.initState();
    // Listen to filter changes and force rebuild
    WidgetsBinding.instance.addPostFrameCallback((_) {
      ref.listenManual(filtersProvider, (previous, next) {
        if (mounted) setState(() {});
      });
    });
  }
</code></pre>
<p>This TabsScreen has a MainDrawer that can open a settings page where filters are updated.  Once returning from that settings page, the filters weren't being applied, but with the above additional code adding a listener to force a rebuild when the filters changed, it finally worked.</p>
